WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.682
This example builds a simple teaching product code.

00:00:02.682 --> 00:00:05.342
BTRIM cleans the product name.

00:00:05.342 --> 00:00:08.057
SUBSTRING takes the first three characters.

00:00:08.057 --> 00:00:12.007
UPPER standardizes the prefix. LPAD pads the numeric ID to three digits.

00:00:12.007 --> 00:00:15.082
The double-pipe operator concatenates the pieces.

00:00:15.082 --> 00:00:22.847
The important lesson is that each part of the code comes from a documented transformation.

00:00:22.847 --> 00:00:27.007
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:27.007 --> 00:00:32.542
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:32.542 --> 00:00:34.307
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:34.307 --> 00:00:39.522
In a real setting such as a CRM standardizing names, emails, and phone numbers,

00:00:39.522 --> 00:00:45.262
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:45.262 --> 00:00:48.362
before discussing syntax.

00:00:48.362 --> 00:00:53.440
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
